Careful now, you mustn't get your facts wrong :-)  It is called MDG, not
MSG as you had mentioned.  Another note about that game... it is a SysV
game, which requires shared memory and semaphores.  If you do not have 
one or the other, then you might as well give up now.  It has been sort
of ported to SunOS, but I would say you have a half and half chance that
it will work on SunOS (even though it might compile fine...) :-)

For what its worth, I am in the process of writing a multi-player game 
which is along the lines of nethack, rogue, etc.  I already have the code
working where multiple players can connect to the game and move around. 
In an older version of the same game that me and a friend were working on,
we actually had surpassed this stage... we had robots (well, they hadn't
actually reached monster stage yet) which moved around and fired guns at
you, players could move around (multi-player of course) and fire guns as
well, and the map could be defined in a variety of ways (maze, cavern, 
room-oriented, etc).  We canned it so that we could begin anew with maybe
better data structures and a little more flexibility in the coding (plus,
we are each working now on our own versions...).

So, as I just gave a hint at what the future could hold.  I will eventually
be taking on idea people where rules could be developed, maps developed,
and maybe I will even take on programmers... but I first want to get the
nutshell of the game done (which I consider 1) players 2) monsters 3) items
4) objects and 5) traps, which are all integrated in some way with the map).

So, give me a week. 

:-)  I first brought up a long time ago about wondering if there was any 
multi-player games available.  So far, these are the suggestions that were
made, and you can find them all on Archie (if you know what that is...):

  1) Myth (which is written in C++, no longer supported, has many problems)
  2) MDG  (which is SysV Unix, need shared memory and semaphores on system)
  3) Crossfire (X-Window game similar to nethack which comes highly highly
	recommended... but it first requires X-Windows, and then secondly
	requires a tremendous amount of disk space...)

Any others out there?  And I also know that there are many out there writing
rogue-like games for fun... but are there anybody working on multi-player
versions?

Catch you later!
